{title: Be Thou My Vision}
{artist: Ancient Irish; tr. Mary E. Byrne (1880-1931); vers. Eleanor H. Hull (1860-1935)}
{key: Eb}
{copyright: Public Domain}

{sov: Verse 1}
[Eb]Be Thou my [Bb]Vision, O [Ab]Lord of my [Eb]heart;
[Bb]Naught be all else to me, [Ab]save that Thou [Bb]art.
[Ab]Thou my best [Eb]Thought, by [Bb]day or by [Cm]night,
[Eb]Waking or [Ab]sleeping, Thy [Bb]presence my [Eb]light.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 2}
[Eb]Be Thou my [Bb]Wisdom, and [Ab]Thou my true [Eb]Word;
[Bb]I ever with Thee and [Ab]Thou with me, [Bb]Lord;
[Ab]Thou my great [Eb]Father, and [Bb]I Thy true [Cm]son,
[Eb]Thou in me [Ab]dwelling, and [Bb]I with Thee [Eb]one.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 3}
[Eb]Riches I [Bb]heed not, nor [Ab]man’s empty [Eb]praise,
[Bb]Thou mine inheritance, [Ab]now and al[Bb]ways;
[Ab]Thou and Thou [Eb]only, first [Bb]in my [Cm]heart,
[Eb]High King of [Ab]heaven, my [Bb]Treasure Thou [Eb]art.
{eov}

{sov: Verse 4}
[Eb]High King of [Bb]heaven, my [Ab]victory [Eb]won,
[Bb]May I reach heaven’s joys, [Ab]O bright heav’n’s [Bb]Sun!
[Ab]Heart of my [Eb]own heart, what[Bb]ever be[Cm]fall,
[Eb]Still be my [Ab]Vision, O [Bb]Ruler of [Eb]all.
{eov}
